id: pm-rl-k5xd
title: "asString returns the untrimmed value, so a padded identity bypasses the contamination gate"
description: "CodeRabbit review of pm-rl#12, Major, Security. asString validates value.trim().length is non-zero but returns the ORIGINAL string. parseScoreRecord therefore accepts evaluation_context of \" env-eval \" with surrounding spaces.\n\nfindContaminationPath compares heldOutEnvironment against entry.spec.environment_version and against run environment ids by STRICT EQUALITY. A padded identity never matches, so the contamination gate passes for a candidate whose training data does reach the evaluation set. The gate is bypassable with a space.\n\nNormalize identities at parse time. Adversarial test: a padded identity must still be caught by the contamination refusal."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-10T23:30:25.979Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-10T23:50:55.103Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-10T23:50:55.086Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-10T23:50:55.086Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-rl-yi7j,parent,"2026-08-10T23:31:10.651Z","harness:claude-code","cli:update:dep",detected
close_reason: "asString now returns the trimmed value, so every identity read through it - environment ids, evaluation contexts, seed sets - is normalized at parse time before any strict-equality comparison. Docstring records why this is a correctness requirement rather than a convenience.\n\nVerified with an ADVERSARIAL test rather than a mutation test: it builds a spec declaring environment_version as two spaces, env-eval, tab, parses it through parseGenerationSpec so normalization happens on the real code path rather than in a hand-built literal, then asserts findContaminationPath still catches it against the held-out id env-eval. Reverting asString to return the raw value turns that test red, 23 of 24 passing; with the fix, 24 of 24.\n\nrelease:check green, coverage still an exact 100/100/100 across 9 files."
body: ""
